 true} [] []\\n   395\\t                      \\\"9007199254740992 18014398509481984 -9007199254740993\\\")\\n   396\\t                 (str \\\"27021597764222973 -9007199254740993 0 -1 \\\"\\n   397\\t                      \\\"-9007199254740993 9007199254740992\\\"))\\n   398\\t               (lines \\\"def ov(f):\\\"\\n   399\\t                      \\\"    try:\\\"\\n   400\\t                      \\\"        return f()\\\"\\n   401\\t                      \\\"    except OverflowError:\\\"\\n   402\\t                      \\\"        return 'overflow'\\\"\\n   403\\t                      (str \\\"print(2 ** 53, ov(lambda: 2 ** 53 + 1), \\\"\\n   404\\t                           \\\"ov(lambda: 3 ** 40), 1 << 52, \\\"\\n   405\\t                           \\\"1 << 53, ov(lambda: 1 << 54), \\\"\\n   406\\t                           \\\"ov(lambda: -(2 ** 53) - 1))\\\")\\n   407\\t                      (str \\\"print(ov(lambda: 9007199254740991 * 3), \\\"\\n   408\\t                           \\\"ov(lambda: -9007199254740992 - 1), \\\"\\n   409\\t                           \\\"0 << 100, -1 >> 100, \\\"\\n   410\\t                           \\\"ov(lambda: ~(2 ** 53)), 1 << 53)\\\")))))\\n   411\\t\\n   412\\t\\n   413\\t(deftest ^:slow gate-p3-round2-test\\n   414\\t  (testing \\\"break and continue in finally swallow the exception in flight\\n   415\\t            (legal since 3.8); raising a non-exception is a TypeError;\\n   416\\t            `except E as e` unbinds e; range len and membership in closed\\n   417\\t            form; dict/set size change during iteration is a RuntimeError;\\n   418\\t            float % and // as CPython's fmod-corrected divmod; builtins take\\n   419\\t            no keywords; a class without __init__ takes no arguments; with\\n   420\\t            looks its dunders up on the type\\\"\\n   421\\t    (every-vm= (prints \\\"after break\\\"\\n   422\\t                       \\\"3\\\"\\n   423\\t                       \\\"TypeError exceptions must derive from BaseException\\\"\\n   424\\t                       \\\"TypeError exceptions must derive from BaseException\\\"\\n   425\\t                       \\\"TypeError exceptions must derive from BaseException\\\"\\n   426\\t                       \\\"TypeError exceptions must derive from BaseException\\\"\\n   427\\t                       \\\"err unbound\\\"\\n   428\\t                       \\\"e2 unbound\\\"\\n   429\\t                       \\\"1501199875790166 True True False 0 4\\\"\\n   430\\t                       \\\"dictionary changed size during iteration\\\"\\n   431\\t                       \\\"Set changed size during iteration\\\"\\n   432\\t                       (str \\\"0.3999999999999999 5.0 0.7000000000000002 -4.0 0.5 \\\"\\n   433\\t                            \\\"0.09999999999999998 1.0000005382899513\\\")\\n   434\\t                       \\\"TypeError\\\"\\n   435\\t                       \\\"TypeError\\\"\\n   436\\t                       \\\"not a context manager\\\")\\n   437\\t               (lines \\\"for i in range(2):\\\"\\n   438\\t                      \\\"    try:\\\"\\n   439\\t                      \\\"        raise ValueError('x')\\\"\\n   440\\t                      \\\"    finally:\\\"\\n   441\\t                      \\\"        break\\\"\\n   442\\t                      \\\"print('after break')\\\"\\n   443\\t                      \\\"n = 0\\\"\\n   444\\t                      \\\"for i in range(3):\\\"\\n   445\\t                      \\\"    try:\\\"\\n   446\\t                      \\\"        raise KeyError('k')\\\"\\n   447\\t                      \\\"    finally:\\\"\\n   448\\t                      \\\"        n += 1\\\"\\n   449\\t                      \\\"        continue\\\"\\n   450\\t                      \\\"print(n)\\\"\\n   451\\t                      \\\"class NotExc:\\\"\\n   452\\t                      \\\"    pass\\\"\\n   453\\t                      \\\"for v in [1, 'x', NotExc, NotExc()]:\\\"\\n   454\\t                      \\\"    try:\\\"\\n   455\\t                      \\\"        raise v\\\"\\n   456\\t                      \\\"    except TypeError as e:\\\"\\n   457\\t                      \\\"        print('TypeError', e.args[0])\\\"\\n   458\\t                      \\\"try:\\\"\\n   459\\t                      \\\"    raise KeyError('k')\\\"\\n   460\\t                      \\\"except KeyError as err:\\\"\\n   461\\t                      \\\"    pass\\\"\\n   462\\t                      \\\"try:\\\"\\n   463\\t                      \\\"    print(err)\\\"\\n   464\\t                      \\\"except NameError:\\\"\\n   465\\t                      \\\"    print('err unbound')\\\"\\n   466\\t                      \\\"def f():\\\"\\n   467\\t                      \\\"    try:\\\"\\n   468\\t                      \\\"        raise KeyError('k')\\\"\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_28\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"All four frontends have a concrete divergence from the full contract. Clojure and Python produce the required condition for fresh escaping exceptions at their public entries; JavaScript and PHP lack that hookup. Foreign reception and unchanged protocol-error rethrow also differ. The local tests pin substantial exception behavior, but I found no real-source language pair exercising §8.10.7 propagation.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_29\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The “same continuation” claim is **false in this snapshot**. Clojure and Python connect fresh escaping exceptions to the supplied raise exit; JavaScript and PHP do not. Foreign reception and protocol-error rethrow also diverge.\\n\\nRead-only audit of `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12`, HEAD `e9a0b430`. No files edited; no tests run. Behavioral consequences below are static deductions from the code.\\n\\n**yang.clojure — DIVERGENT.**\\n\\n- **Lowering:** `throw` becomes `[:yin.k/error payload]`. `try` examines that outcome and binds its payload to the handler. It accepts exactly one `Exception`/`ExceptionInfo` catch, performs no runtime class discrimination, and rejects `finally`. See [clojure.cljc:904](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:904).\\n- **Raise-exit hookup:** the public entry reads `:yang.call/raise` at line 1070 and invokes it with `(yang.call/thrown payload)` at [clojure.cljc:1088](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1088). Fresh public escapes therefore have the specified condition shape.\\n- **Private path:** intra-unit calls pass `[compiler-token args]` and receive outcomes directly; authenticated outcome-token calls also bypass public value/raise conversion. See [clojure.cljc:1110](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1110), [clojure.cljc:1486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1486). Thus its internal exception lowering is an ordinary result channel, unlike the other frontends’ handler continuations.\\n- **Foreign reception:** `compile-guest-call` supplies a capture-backed raise closure. A delivered `:yang.call/thrown` becomes an error outcome containing the **raw payload**; another reason becomes an error outcome containing the **whole condition as data**. See [clojure.cljc:1297](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1297). Source catches receive that data. Rethrowing a protocol condition subsequently reaches the public entry’s unconditional `yang.call/thrown`, changing its reason rather than forwarding the original condition.\\n- **Root:** `clj/run-main` unwraps successful outcomes and leaves error outcomes as reporting values; it supplies no invocation raise exit. See [clojure/prelude.cljc:87](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:87).\\n\\n**yang.typescript / JavaScript — DIVERGENT.**\\n\\n- **Lowering:** `throw` calls `js/throw`; `try/catch` calls `js/try`; an enclosing `finally` calls `js/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:2165](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:2165), line 2214.\\n- **Local continuation:** `js/try` captures a guest continuation and installs it in `js.rt/ctx`; `js/throw` walks that runtime’s handler/finally stack. These are guest continuations, not host-native `try/catch`. See [prelude.cljc:1704](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1704).\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: absent for escaping source exceptions.** The published function entry directly applies `:code`; it neither reads `:yang.call/raise` nor installs a per-activation exception bridge. See [prelude.cljc:1581](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1581). An escaping throw therefore follows existing JS handlers rather than the foreign caller’s supplied exit. With no JS handler, it reaches the `:js/no-handler` expression.\\n- **Foreign reception:** `js/call` really does deliver `:yang.call/value` raw through `js/throw`. However, protocol failures become newly constructed `TypeError`s containing only selected text; the original condition is discarded. See [prelude.cljc:1627](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1627). Unchanged protocol-error rethrow cannot recover it.\\n- **Root:** `js/run-main` installs a JS root handler and snapshots uncaught values; it does not repair the missing callee bridge. See [prelude.cljc:1850](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1850).\\n\\n**yang.php — DIVERGENT.**\\n\\n- **Lowering:** source throws call `php/throw-value`; catches dispatch through local `php/instance-of`; unmatched catches call `php/raise`; `finally` becomes `php/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:1889](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:1889), line 3600. `throw-value` requires a local Throwable and then raises it. See [prelude.cljc:4084](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4084).\\n- **Local continuation:** `php/try` captures a guest handler continuation; `php/raise` walks PHP’s handler/finally stack. With an empty stack, `php/raise` **returns the error normally**. See [prelude.cljc:190](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:190).\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: absent for source/binding exceptions.** The function entry performs named-argument placement and applies `:code` without an exception bridge. Its supplied raise exit is used only for unsupported binding profiles. See [prelude.cljc:2264](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2264), line 2281. The Closure entry likewise ignores the supplied exit and delegates through `php/call`: [prelude.cljc:4217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4217).\\n- **Foreign reception:** `php/call`, `call-with-binding-profile`, and `call-named` convert not-callable to a fresh local `Error`; otherwise they call **`php/raise condition`**, without extracting `:yang.call/value`. See [prelude.cljc:2384](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2384), line 4314. Even an original PHP Throwable arriving inside a valid thrown condition is therefore delivered as its envelope, not restored.\\n- **`ForeignException`: nowhere.** No wrapper, local hierarchy, or identity-owned retained-condition association exists. `catch (Throwable)` receives no valid local wrapper; explicitly throwing the envelope cannot preserve the original condition.\\n- **Root:** `php/run-main` catches through PHP’s local stack and snapshots the result. See [prelude.cljc:5070](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5070).\\n\\n**yang.python — DIVERGENT.**\\n\\n- **Lowering:** `raise X` calls `py/as-exception` then `py/raise`; bare rethrow uses the captured exception directly. Typed clauses use `py/exc-matches`, bare `except` selects its handler unconditionally, and `finally` becomes `py/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:1664](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:1664), lines 1699 and 1759.\\n- **Local continuation:** `py/try` captures and installs a guest handler, restoring saved context before calling its handler; `py/raise` runs intervening finally frames. See [prelude.cljc:294](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:294).\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: present.** `py/function-entry` reads the supplied exit at line 893 and places **both argument binding and body execution** inside `py/try`. Its handler invokes that exit with the required `:yang.call/thrown`/`:yang.call/value` condition. See [prelude.cljc:886](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:886), line 912. Fresh escaping Python exceptions and binding failures follow the intended boundary path.\\n- **Foreign reception:** `py/call-kw` delivers every thrown payload directly through `py/raise`, without validating whether it is a local exception or wrapping foreign data. Protocol failures become a generic `TypeError`, losing the condition. See [prelude.cljc:1034](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1034).\\n- **`ForeignException`: nowhere.** Bare `except` can receive raw foreign data, but typed catches use `py/type-of` and the local hierarchy; foreign data has no generated local exception class. Explicit `raise e` applies `py/as-exception` and can replace that foreign payload with a `TypeError`. See [prelude.cljc:352](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:352), lines 754 and 802. There is no wrapper-lifetime condition association to recover in another activation.\\n- **Root:** `py/run-main` catches and snapshots through Python’s local stack. See [prelude.cljc:3322](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:3322).\\n\\n**Local coverage inventory — assertions present, not rerun.**\\n\\n| Frontend | What its own tests pin |\\n|---|---|\\n| Clojure | Throw/error outcomes: [clojure_test.clj:1162](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1162). Broad admitted catch, nested replacement throw, operand escape, and rejection of `finally`/`Throwable`: [line 2452](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2452). Named binding failure emits `:yang.call/thrown`: line 1429. No unchanged-rethrow identity assertion found in these cases. |\\n| JavaScript | Object and primitive throws, catch-all, finally on normal/return exits, string rethrow, uncaught exceptions: [e2e_test.clj:254](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/e2e_test.clj:254). Finally ordering/escape cleanup: [prelude_parity_test.cljc:677](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:677), line 785. Typed catch syntax is inapplicable. |\\n| PHP | Throwable hierarchy, typed/multi-type catches, `Throwable` catch-all, unmatched propagation, rethrow, finally/return override and exception replacement: [exceptions_test.clj:19](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/php/antlr/exceptions_test.clj:19), lines 33, 48 and 63. The test labelled “rethrow keeps the same object” checks its message, **not object identity**. |\\n| Python | Typed catches across a local callee, bare rethrow, uncaught errors: [e2e_test.clj:534](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_test.clj:534), lines 554 and 609. Bare catch-all lowering: [lower_test.clj:197](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/lower_test.clj:197). Finally on every exit, nested cleanup, return override and replacement exception: [e2e_c1_test.clj:30](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c1_test.clj:30), lines 62 and 91. Binder condition shape: [call_entry_test.cljc:76](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/call_entry_test.cljc:76). |\\n\\n**Cross-language evidence: the “zero exception legs” report is confirmed for the checked-in conformance tests.**\\n\\nThe shared kit composes donor exports and runs caller source; it contains no exception scenario itself: [conformance.clj:245](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/call/conformance.clj:245). Its real-source consumers in [cross_lang_test.clj:26](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj:26), Python’s [source_call_test.clj:9](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/source_call_test.clj:9), and TS/PHP’s [source_call_test.cljc:98](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/source_call_test.cljc:98) exercise successful calls, callbacks, identity and opaque values—not escaping exceptions.\\n\\nThere **is** a JavaScript reception fixture asserting payload identity after a hand-built entry invokes the supplied raise exit: [prelude_parity_test.cljc:768](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:768). That is useful helper evidence, but no second frontend compiles the throw. Likewise, shared helper tests construct conditions directly: [call_test.cljc:157](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/core/call_test.cljc:157).\\n\\nConsequently, no real-source language pair currently establishes the §8.10.7 propagation contract, including binding-failure propagation, foreign typed/catch-all reception, unchanged/protocol rethrow, finally replacement, A→B→A cleanup and identity, or saved `ForeignException` rethrow from another activation.\\n\\n**Ranked divergences:**\\n\\n1. **Missing callee exception bridges in JavaScript and PHP.** Their entries bypass the supplied raise exit for source exceptions; PHP binding failures also occur outside such a bridge. Evidence: JS prelude line 1581; PHP prelude lines 2264 and 4217.\\n2. **Missing foreign-exception reception in Python and PHP.** Python raises raw foreign payloads; PHP raises the entire condition envelope. Neither implements `ForeignException` or its lifetime-owned condition association. Evidence: Python prelude line 1034; PHP prelude line 2384.\\n3. **Original protocol conditions do not survive unchanged rethrow.** JS/Python discard them while constructing local errors; Clojure retains them as catch data but wraps a rethrow in a new thrown condition; PHP supplies an envelope without a valid local wrapper. Evidence: JS line 1634; Python line 1046; Clojure lines 1306 and 1088; PHP lines 2391 and 4085.\\n4. **Clojure has a distinct internal failure channel and restricted catch/finally lowering.** Error vectors and authenticated private calls carry failures as ordinary results before public conversion. Evidence: compiler lines 904, 1110 and 1486.\\n\\nI found no synthesized engine-effect exception value, guessed foreign class-name matching, or source-map detector that forwards data merely because it looks like a condition. Dispatch on the reason of an actually delivered boundary condition is distinct from that forbidden shape inference.\\n\\n**Overall verdict: DIVERGENT.**\"}}\n{\"type\":\"turn.completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1190399,\"cached_input_tokens\":1069312,\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":10086,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":2837}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
