{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"agent_message","text":"**ACCEPT WITH AMENDMENTS.** The core decision matches §8.10.7, but the ruling needs corrections to its scope, divergence accounting, sequencing, and completion gate.\n\nReviewed the ruling and the audit’s last agent message against `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12`, HEAD `e9a0b430`. No files edited; no tests run.\n\n1. **R3 must explicitly cover all four frontends and require outward forwarding.**\n\n   The baseline summary at ruling lines 59–60 understates the audit’s third divergence. Besides JS/Python discarding conditions:\n\n   - Clojure retains a protocol condition as catch data at [clojure.cljc:1306](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1306), then reclassifies its rethrow through unconditional `yang.call/thrown` at line 1088.\n   - PHP constructs a fresh `Error` for not-callable and otherwise raises the envelope at [prelude.cljc:2384](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2384); source rethrow rejects that envelope at line 4085.\n\n   Assign R3 fixes to Clojure/PHP explicitly. Require an unchanged generated exception to forward the **complete original condition, including its qualified reason and evidence**, rather than merely retain enough information that it “can” forward.\n\n   Also correct `:invalid-invocation` and `:unsupported-binding` to `:yang.call/invalid-invocation` and `:yang.call/unsupported-binding`.\n\n2. **Distinguish fresh exceptions from retained-condition rethrows.**\n\n   The opening and R1 literally require every escape to become `:yang.call/thrown`. That conflicts with R3 and §8.10.7 when an unchanged generated protocol exception escapes: its original protocol reason must survive.\n\n   Amend this to: fresh source exceptions and binding failures produce a thrown condition; unchanged rethrows of generated retained-condition exceptions forward their original condition.\n\n   R2 should also state the required lifetime mechanism: association with the generated exception’s identity, separate from source-writable attributes, surviving its creating activation, without an ambient registry. [The design requires this explicitly.](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3885) “Retaining” plus one stored-wrapper scenario leaves room for an incomplete implementation.\n\n3. **Repair the fix order; it omits R1 JS and can regress local PHP catches.**\n\n   Ruling line 76 schedules R1 PHP but never schedules the missing JS bridge.\n\n   There are also concrete dependencies:\n\n   - R3 JS retention alone cannot establish cross-boundary rethrow while the JS entry ignores the supplied exit.\n   - Python’s R2/R3 work must update the outbound bridge at line 912; that bridge currently wraps every escape in a fresh thrown condition.\n   - Landing R1 PHP before reception repair redirects local PHP exceptions through the existing `php/call` receiver, which raises the **condition envelope**. A previously working typed local catch can consequently stop matching.\n   - PHP’s Closure entry must preserve the incoming exception path; simply delegating through its existing `php/call` creates an inner exit while ignoring the supplied one.\n\n   Establish condition retention/forwarding first or alongside each frontend’s bridge. Land PHP R1/R2/R3 as a compatible change, add the missing JS R1 round, and explicitly schedule Clojure R3. R4’s internal optimization decision can remain last; Clojure’s public protocol-rethrow repair need not wait.\n\n4. **The acceptance gate is insufficient as written.**\n\n   Adding exception scenarios to the current kit does not establish the required coverage:\n\n   - **Pairs:** derive all **12 directed cross-frontend pairs** from the four admitted frontends. Existing source tests cover eight directions; Clojure↔Python and Clojure↔PHP must not disappear behind “admitted pair.”\n   - **VMs and hosts:** [the kit](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/call/conformance.clj:23) runs linked programs on semantic, stack, and register VMs. Its `.clj` runner does not supply Node/Dart coverage or the admitted bundled AST-walker path. Require portable packet fixtures, JVM/Node/Dart execution, and an explicit host-by-VM matrix. Keep the current linked-walker refusal explicit; do not require unsupported linked execution.\n   - **Context lifetime:** add repeated success→raise→success, cross-runtime recursion, and suspension/resume tests proving handler/depth/frame restoration and exactly-once cleanup. These are separate [§8.10.11 obligations](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:4064).\n   - **Callable kinds:** include actual PHP Closures and higher-order entries, not only named function exports.\n   - **Assertions:** check original reasons, complete evidence, payload identity, wrapper non-stacking, and cleanup order—not only rendered output.\n\n   Apply scenarios according to source capabilities. Requiring typed JS catches or Clojure `finally` would contradict the source semantics R4 expressly preserves.\n\n5. **Correct the factual wording and sharpen two citations.**\n\n   All requested load-bearing locations exist at this HEAD, and the main audit conclusions check out:\n\n   | Checked locations | Result |\n   |---|---|\n   | JS `1581`, `1627` | Missing entry bridge; protocol condition discarded |\n   | PHP `190`, `2264`, `2384`, `4217` | Empty-stack normal return; missing function/Closure bridges; envelope reception |\n   | Python `886–912`, `1034`, `1046` | Binding/body bridge present; raw thrown-payload reception; protocol condition discarded |\n   | Clojure `904`, `1088`, `1110`, `1486` | Error-outcome lowering; public raise conversion; private token path |\n\n   Specific corrections:\n\n   - **“host engine’s own handler stack” is wrong.** JS uses its generated guest continuation stack at [prelude.cljc:1704](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1704), not host-native exception handlers. The defect is bypassing the activation boundary.\n   - PHP `2384` supports the envelope-reception claim; the direct empty-stack return is at **194–197**. Keep these distinct.\n   - Clojure `1110` is the packet-length guard. The actual bypass is **1112–1115**; the additional outcome-token bypass is **1125–1132**.\n   - R2’s “raw payload … is non-compliant” needs **“otherwise foreign”**: delivering an already valid local exception unchanged is required.\n\n   I found no stale or unrelated citation among the other requested anchors.\n\nEach requirement is testable, with these focused pins:\n\n| Requirement | Focused evidence |\n|---|---|\n| R1 | Real lowered function and closure: body throw and binding failure reach a non-returning supplied exit exactly once; execution after the call is unreachable |\n| R2 | Foreign payload gets a local wrapper; local exception remains identical; saved-wrapper rethrow restores the original condition after its creating activation ends |\n| R3 | Deliver each qualified protocol reason, catch/rethrow its generated local exception, and assert the complete original condition; focused fixtures can construct invalid invocations |\n| R4 | Compare public and optimized paths across static, dynamic, tail, higher-order, and foreign re-entry cases; a single passing example is insufficient equivalence evidence |\n| R5 | Returned condition-shaped map remains normal data; thrown lookalike becomes a fresh payload; fabricated or modified source attributes cannot authenticate a retained condition |\n\nR4’s optimization allowance is consistent with the design. Preserving Clojure’s restricted catch surface is also correct. The amendments should strengthen boundary equivalence without mandating identical internal lowering or broad interception of VM/custody/host implementation failures."}}
